In this first installment, I've collected several short audio clips, each s=
ix minutes or less, for you to listen to. The Free Lunch is Over: A Fundame=
ntal Turn  Towards Concurrency in Software, Dr.
The third is the OpenMP group, represented by Michael Wong of IBM  and Bron=
is R.
Creators of dynamic language engines are in the business of taking, say, Ru=
by code and turning that into Java bytecode. It provides for resource  inje=
ction as well type conversion, and even for string substitution. We're repl=
icating critical bits of the heap where the developer tells us to.
If we put classes and templates in the interface, there is no  easy way for=
 programmers to use those from straight C code. If you can remove that from=
 automatically going into the database, then you also get a great benefit o=
f simplicity because now you're dealing in pure Java objects. In many dynam=
ic languages, methods can be changed, and even added to a class, at runtime=
. One of Terracotta's promises is an easier programming model for clustered=
 applications.
The visual properties are automatically applied to the Action's component, =
and some  of them, like the enabled property, affect both the component's a=
ppearance and its  behavior.
In fact, the number of distinct widget values is rather small. What's uniqu=
e about Terracotta's clustering?
Threads and Exceptions What happens if a thread terminates because of an un=
caught exception? So they flush things to a database for reliability or dur=
ability. Ari Zilka: Spring closes some gaps between what an enterprise deve=
loper needs and what pure Java provides.
